1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,990
For thirty-three years, Nvidia has had one boss.

2
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:09,550
Last night, his own board met to decide whether to fire him.

3
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:11,949
Jensen Huang founded the company.

4
00:00:11,779 --> 00:00:15,310
He is the face of the entire AI boom.

5
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:18,510
But he owns only about three and a half percent of it.

6
00:00:18,339 --> 00:00:20,750
He has no special shares.

7
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:28,910
And under Nvidia's own rules, a simple majority of the board can remove him as CEO, with or without a reason.

8
00:00:28,739 --> 00:00:30,589
There are eleven directors.

9
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:32,590
It takes six votes.

10
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:38,189
When the report came out, after the market had closed, the stock fell more than eight percent.

11
00:00:38,259 --> 00:00:42,509
And here is the part that should worry anyone with a retirement account.

12
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:45,630
There is no plan for who comes next.

13
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:51,390
This is what happens to the most important company in AI if the man who built it is gone.

14
00:00:52,198 --> 00:00:53,969
A quick note before we start.

15
00:00:54,279 --> 00:00:56,889
This was recorded on November 12th.

16
00:00:56,918 --> 00:01:00,209
The board had met, but had not announced a decision.

17
00:01:00,518 --> 00:01:05,569
If that has changed by the time you watch this, everything that follows still applies.

18
00:01:05,879 --> 00:01:10,688
Because the real question is no longer whether Jensen Huang survives this week.

19
00:01:10,918 --> 00:01:13,968
It is whether Nvidia can run without him.

20
00:01:14,772 --> 00:01:17,902
Here is what has been reported, and by whom.

21
00:01:18,292 --> 00:01:25,341
On November 8th, Nvidia said its board had opened an independent investigation into its CEO.

22
00:01:25,171 --> 00:01:36,861
That followed reports that Huang had a relationship with an executive at a company that buys Nvidia's chips, and that American officials believe she was working with Chinese intelligence.

23
00:01:36,691 --> 00:01:38,462
China denies it.

24
00:01:38,251 --> 00:01:43,822
Huang told employees, in his words, "There is no excuse for what I did."

25
00:01:43,652 --> 00:01:46,221
At first, the board backed him.

26
00:01:46,052 --> 00:01:52,061
It said it had confidence in Jensen, and no reason to believe company secrets were compromised.

27
00:01:51,971 --> 00:01:59,262
Then, this week, the New York Times reported a second relationship, this time with someone who worked for him.

28
00:01:59,052 --> 00:02:03,542
And the Wall Street Journal reported that the board was preparing to remove him.

29
00:02:03,572 --> 00:02:06,861
Those are reports, from people familiar with the matter.

30
00:02:07,091 --> 00:02:09,262
Nvidia has not confirmed them.

31
00:02:09,491 --> 00:02:12,861
And the spying claim in particular has not been proven.

32
00:02:13,092 --> 00:02:15,901
We are not going to dwell on the personal story.

33
00:02:16,132 --> 00:02:20,062
The question for anyone who owns this stock is a different one.

34
00:02:20,864 --> 00:02:25,593
Most giant companies are built so that no single person matters too much.

35
00:02:25,984 --> 00:02:28,473
Nvidia was built the opposite way.

36
00:02:28,783 --> 00:02:32,713
A typical CEO has maybe ten people reporting to them.

37
00:02:33,024 --> 00:02:35,954
Jensen Huang has more than fifty.

38
00:02:36,144 --> 00:02:37,354
No layers.

39
00:02:37,264 --> 00:02:38,633
No deputy.

40
00:02:38,704 --> 00:02:41,593
Every big decision goes through one desk.

41
00:02:41,424 --> 00:02:44,153
And the decisions were his.

42
00:02:44,144 --> 00:02:50,394
Twenty years ago he bet the company on software that let its gaming chips do scientific math.

43
00:02:50,383 --> 00:02:52,554
Wall Street hated it.

44
00:02:52,463 --> 00:02:57,193
That bet is the reason Nvidia owns the AI chip market today.

45
00:02:57,024 --> 00:03:03,114
So when investors paid five trillion dollars for this company, they were not only buying chips.

46
00:03:02,943 --> 00:03:05,514
They were buying his judgment.

47
00:03:05,503 --> 00:03:12,713
Since the investigation was announced, more than four hundred and fifty billion dollars of that value has disappeared.

48
00:03:12,623 --> 00:03:14,234
And counting.

49
00:03:14,223 --> 00:03:17,993
That is the price the market is putting on one person.

50
00:03:18,806 --> 00:03:21,137
So can the board really do it?

51
00:03:21,447 --> 00:03:22,377
Yes.

52
00:03:22,326 --> 00:03:24,016
And this surprises people.

53
00:03:24,326 --> 00:03:29,616
At Meta, Mark Zuckerberg holds special shares that give him control of the votes.

54
00:03:29,847 --> 00:03:31,937
The board cannot fire him.

55
00:03:32,247 --> 00:03:34,417
Nvidia has nothing like that.

56
00:03:34,727 --> 00:03:37,657
Huang's three and a half percent makes him rich.

57
00:03:37,606 --> 00:03:39,537
It does not make him safe.

58
00:03:40,086 --> 00:03:41,776
There is one twist.

59
00:03:42,086 --> 00:03:44,336
The board can take his job.

60
00:03:44,567 --> 00:03:46,977
It cannot take his seat at the table.

61
00:03:47,447 --> 00:03:50,977
Removing him as a director would need a vote of the shareholders.

62
00:03:51,127 --> 00:03:54,817
So even if he is fired, he could still be in the room.

63
00:03:55,206 --> 00:03:58,977
There is also a reason the board cannot simply wait this out.

64
00:03:59,287 --> 00:04:03,776
Nvidia needs permission from Washington to sell its best chips abroad.

65
00:04:04,086 --> 00:04:09,056
A CEO under a cloud about China is a problem for exactly that.

66
00:04:09,366 --> 00:04:13,856
A senior member of Congress has already said he is prepared to subpoena him.

67
00:04:14,247 --> 00:04:17,137
So this is not only about a private life.

68
00:04:17,286 --> 00:04:23,776
It is about whether the United States government still trusts the person running its most important chip company.

69
00:04:24,586 --> 00:04:26,435
So who could take the job?

70
00:04:26,745 --> 00:04:28,516
There are three kinds of answer.

71
00:04:28,906 --> 00:04:30,036
The engineer.

72
00:04:30,346 --> 00:04:33,956
Ian Buck joined Nvidia in 2004.

73
00:04:33,745 --> 00:04:43,716
He was a key architect of the software that made the AI boom possible, and today he runs the part of Nvidia that sells to the biggest AI customers.

74
00:04:43,545 --> 00:04:48,355
If you want someone who understands why Nvidia wins, it is him.

75
00:04:48,305 --> 00:04:51,156
But he has never run a whole company.

76
00:04:50,946 --> 00:04:52,355
The operator.

77
00:04:52,146 --> 00:04:54,836
Colette Kress, the finance chief.

78
00:04:54,665 --> 00:04:57,396
Steady, trusted by Wall Street.

79
00:04:57,346 --> 00:05:01,795
According to one report, some directors favor her as a temporary CEO.

80
00:05:02,105 --> 00:05:03,555
The outsider.

81
00:05:03,466 --> 00:05:08,836
Names like Lisa Su, who runs rival AMD, or Rene Haas of Arm.

82
00:05:08,985 --> 00:05:10,836
Proven chief executives.

83
00:05:11,065 --> 00:05:15,956
But it would mean handing the keys to someone who was not there for any of it.

84
00:05:15,786 --> 00:05:18,755
And then there is the answer Newsweek put in a headline.

85
00:05:19,065 --> 00:05:20,915
Who could replace Jensen Huang?

86
00:05:21,305 --> 00:05:22,355
Nobody.

87
00:05:23,156 --> 00:05:24,685
That sounds bleak.

88
00:05:24,995 --> 00:05:29,365
So let us look at what actually happened to three other companies.

89
00:05:29,475 --> 00:05:31,886
Intel, 2018.

90
00:05:31,716 --> 00:05:36,125
The CEO was pushed out over a relationship with an employee.

91
00:05:35,915 --> 00:05:38,766
The stock barely moved that day.

92
00:05:38,596 --> 00:05:43,406
But over the next few years, Intel lost its lead in making chips.

93
00:05:43,235 --> 00:05:45,805
The trouble was already underway.

94
00:05:45,636 --> 00:05:49,805
Losing the boss did not cause it, and did not fix it.

95
00:05:49,636 --> 00:05:52,125
McDonald's, 2019.

96
00:05:51,995 --> 00:05:55,326
Same reason, same outcome for the CEO.

97
00:05:55,156 --> 00:05:57,886
The stock dropped about five percent.

98
00:05:57,795 --> 00:06:02,286
Then the new boss carried on, and the shares went on to new highs.

99
00:06:02,355 --> 00:06:03,485
And Apple.

100
00:06:03,475 --> 00:06:09,565
In 2011, Steve Jobs stepped down, and plenty of people said the company was finished.

101
00:06:09,396 --> 00:06:13,045
His replacement was an operations man named Tim Cook.

102
00:06:13,076 --> 00:06:16,286
Apple is worth about ten times more today.

103
00:06:16,596 --> 00:06:18,125
The lesson is simple.

104
00:06:18,276 --> 00:06:25,005
It depends on whether the company's edge lives in one person's head, or in the system that person built.

105
00:06:25,903 --> 00:06:27,913
So here is what to look for.

106
00:06:28,303 --> 00:06:28,874
One.

107
00:06:29,103 --> 00:06:32,954
Who gets named, and whether it is "interim" or permanent.

108
00:06:33,184 --> 00:06:36,793
An interim boss means the board does not have a plan yet.

109
00:06:37,184 --> 00:06:38,033
Two.

110
00:06:38,064 --> 00:06:39,913
Whether the engineers stay.

111
00:06:40,223 --> 00:06:42,553
Nvidia's lead is its people.

112
00:06:42,704 --> 00:06:47,754
If the top technical names start leaving, that tells you more than any press release.

113
00:06:48,064 --> 00:06:48,793
Three.

114
00:06:49,024 --> 00:06:50,634
November 17th.

115
00:06:50,863 --> 00:06:52,954
Nvidia reports its earnings.

116
00:06:53,103 --> 00:06:58,954
It will be the first set of results delivered under this cloud, and possibly by someone new.

117
00:06:59,024 --> 00:07:05,834
For thirty years, Jensen Huang told his employees the company was thirty days from going out of business.

118
00:07:05,624 --> 00:07:08,793
He was trying to prepare them for everything.

119
00:07:08,663 --> 00:07:12,793
He never told them what to do if the thing they lost was him.
